      "rev_n_1_d962e7_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_d962e7_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_d962e7",
        "thought_text": "Calculate the median of the x-coordinates and y-coordinates separately. The intersection at the median will minimize the Manhattan distance for all citizens without considering the distance constraint d. This provides a baseline solution.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794409125.642669
      },
      "rev_n_2_354ea6_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_354ea6_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_354ea6",
        "thought_text": "Sort the citizens based on their x and y coordinates. Then, iterate through possible intersections by considering the median as the starting point and expanding outwards while checking if all citizens are within distance d. This approach balances minimizing distance and respecting the constraint.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794409125.642675
      },
      "rev_n_3_bf5208_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_bf5208_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_bf5208",
        "thought_text": "Use a binary search approach to find the optimal intersection. Start with the minimum and maximum possible x and y values, and iteratively narrow down the search space by checking if the current midpoint satisfies the distance constraint for all citizens.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794409125.642679
      },
